INT. NANA ROSALBA'S KITCHEN - POWER OUT - NIGHT

Candles in jam jars. A pot of rice losing its heat on a dead stove. Rain on the tin roof, loud as applause.

NANA ROSALBA, 80s, in a housecoat, ladles soup by feel. Her son DESMOND, 50s, kneels at the breaker box with a phone flashlight in his teeth. His daughter IMANI, 16, scrolls a dead phone in disgust. KOFI, 9, hides under the table with a toy truck. AUNT PETRA, 60s, wrings her hands by the window.

DESMOND
(around the phone)
Everybody stay put.

PETRA
We are staying put, Desmond, there is nowhere to go.

IMANI
(to herself)
Twelve percent. I had twelve percent.

NANA
(to the room)
Bowls. Whoever is standing, get bowls.

KOFI
(from under the table)
It's too dark to find the bowls.

NANA
Then find them with your hands, like Jesus.

IMANI
Nana --

DESMOND
Mama, he didn't --

PETRA
It's in the Bible somewhere.

Thunder cracks. The candles gutter. Kofi shrieks. Everyone talks at once, and then all stop.

Nana places a bowl in front of her grandson, then touches his hair.

NANA
(softly)
Eat.
